## Proof

**Setup and basic facts.**

The density $f(x)=|x|^{-3}\mathbf{1}_{|x|>1}$ is valid since
$$\int_{|x|>1}|x|^{-3}\,dx = 2\int_1^\infty x^{-3}\,dx = 2\cdot\tfrac12 = 1.$$
It is symmetric, so $\mathbb{E}[X_1]=0$. Note $\mathbb{E}[X_1^2]=2\int_1^\infty x^{-1}\,dx=\infty$: the variance is infinite, so the ordinary CLT does not apply. The tail is
$$\mathbb{P}(|X_1|>t)=2\int_t^\infty x^{-3}\,dx = t^{-2},\qquad t\ge 1.$$

**Truncation and its second moment.**

Set $b_n:=\sqrt{n\log n}$ and define the truncated variable
$$\bar X_k := X_k\,\mathbf{1}_{\{|X_k|\le b_n\}}.$$
By symmetry $\mathbb{E}[\bar X_k]=0$, and
$$\mathbb{E}[\bar X_k^2]=2\int_1^{b_n} x^2\cdot x^{-3}\,dx = 2\int_1^{b_n}\frac{dx}{x}=2\log b_n = \log(n\log n)=\log n+\log\log n.$$

**Lindeberg CLT for the truncated sum (the given fact).**

Consider the triangular array $Y_{n,k}:=\bar X_k/\sqrt{n\log n}$, $1\le k\le n$. Its row variance is
$$s_n^2=\sum_{k=1}^n\operatorname{Var}(Y_{n,k})=\frac{n\,\mathbb{E}[\bar X_1^2]}{n\log n}=\frac{\log n+\log\log n}{\log n}\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}1.$$

We verify Lindeberg's condition. For any $\varepsilon>0$,
$$\frac{1}{s_n^2}\sum_{k=1}^n\mathbb{E}\!\left[Y_{n,k}^2\,\mathbf{1}_{\{|Y_{n,k}|>\varepsilon s_n\}}\right]
=\frac{n}{s_n^2}\cdot\frac{1}{n\log n}\,\mathbb{E}\!\left[\bar X_1^2\,\mathbf{1}_{\{|\bar X_1|>\varepsilon s_n\sqrt{n\log n}\}}\right].$$
Since $|\bar X_1|\le b_n=\sqrt{n\log n}$ and $s_n\to1$, the threshold $\varepsilon s_n\sqrt{n\log n}$ is eventually $\le b_n$ (when $\varepsilon<1$; for $\varepsilon\ge1$ the indicator is eventually $0$). In the nontrivial case,
$$\mathbb{E}\!\left[\bar X_1^2\,\mathbf{1}_{\{\varepsilon s_n\sqrt{n\log n}<|\bar X_1|\le b_n\}}\right]
=2\int_{\varepsilon s_n\sqrt{n\log n}}^{b_n}\frac{dx}{x}
=2\log\!\frac{b_n}{\varepsilon s_n\sqrt{n\log n}}
=2\log\frac{1}{\varepsilon s_n}\to 2\log\frac1\varepsilon,$$
a bounded constant. Hence the Lindeberg sum is
$$\frac{1}{s_n^2\log n}\cdot O(1)\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}0.$$
Lindeberg's condition holds, so by the Lindeberg CLT
$$\frac{\sum_{k=1}^n \bar X_k}{\sqrt{n\log n}}\xrightarrow{d}N(0,1).$$

**The untruncated sum has the same limit.**

Let $T_n:=\sum_{k=1}^n X_k$ and $\bar T_n:=\sum_{k=1}^n\bar X_k$. The difference is
$$T_n-\bar T_n=\sum_{k=1}^n X_k\,\mathbf{1}_{\{|X_k|>b_n\}}.$$
We show this difference is $o_{\mathbb{P}}(\sqrt{n\log n})$. Using the tail bound $\mathbb{P}(|X_1|>t)=t^{-2}$ and a union bound,
$$\mathbb{P}(T_n\neq \bar T_n)\le\mathbb{P}\!\left(\max_{1\le k\le n}|X_k|>b_n\right)\le n\,\mathbb{P}(|X_1|>b_n)=n\cdot b_n^{-2}=n\cdot\frac{1}{n\log n}=\frac{1}{\log n}\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}0.$$
Thus $T_n=\bar T_n$ with probability tending to $1$, and in particular
$$\frac{T_n-\bar T_n}{\sqrt{n\log n}}\xrightarrow{\mathbb{P}}0.$$

**Conclusion via Slutsky's theorem.**

Writing
$$\frac{T_n}{\sqrt{n\log n}}=\frac{\bar T_n}{\sqrt{n\log n}}+\frac{T_n-\bar T_n}{\sqrt{n\log n}},$$
the first term converges in distribution to $N(0,1)$ and the second converges in probability to $0$. By Slutsky's theorem,
$$\frac{1}{\sqrt{n\log n}}\sum_{k=1}^n X_k \xrightarrow{d} N(0,1).$$

Therefore the limit is $N(0,\sigma^2)$ with
$$\boxed{\sigma^2=1,\qquad \sigma=1.}$$

